From: Armada <noreply@armada.local>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: cq8725s-ayn: declare a control TCS on the apps RSC

rpmh_rsc_write_next_wakeup() only programs the DRV's CTL_TCS_DATA
registers when the DT declares a CONTROL_TCS, and sm8750.dtsi declares
none. The AOP therefore never learns when the application processor
expects to wake. On the AYN Odin 3 the cores enter apps-pc for the
whole of every suspend, every AP-side vote is released, and the SoC
still never enters CX shutdown or AOSS deep sleep, with DDR parked at
its last active frequency, in both s2idle and PSCI system suspend.

Declare one control TCS. The driver assigns it no hardware slot, so the
eight-TCS layout is unchanged; it only enables the next-wakeup write at
each flush.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/cq8725s-ayn-common.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/cq8725s-ayn-common.dtsi
@@ -341,6 +341,17 @@
 };
 
 &apps_rsc {
+	/*
+	 * Declare a control TCS so rpmh_rsc_write_next_wakeup() programs the
+	 * next expected wakeup into the DRV's CTL_TCS_DATA registers at every
+	 * flush. With CONTROL_TCS 0 the driver never tells the AOP when the AP
+	 * will wake, and on the Odin 3 the AOP then never attempts cxsd/aosd:
+	 * the cores sleep, every AP vote is released, and DDR sits at its last
+	 * active frequency. The control TCS takes no hardware slot.
+	 */
+	qcom,tcs-config = <ACTIVE_TCS 3>, <SLEEP_TCS 2>,
+			  <WAKE_TCS 2>, <CONTROL_TCS 1>;
+
 	regulators-0 {
 		compatible = "qcom,pm8550-rpmh-regulators";
 
